# Portal 2 (Source engine, PE32/i386) -- appid 620.
#
# The title dies before loading a single Source DLL, in tier0's thread pool,
# with its own message box:
#
#     Error!
#     Out of thread ids. Decrease the number of threads or increase
#     MAX_THREAD_IDS
#
# [MEASURED 2026-08-30, run 20260830-221723] That box is the whole failure.
# No engine.dll, no filesystem_stdio.dll beyond a BuiltDebug probe, and
# steamclient.dll never looked for -- so the Steam integration this title was
# assumed to be blocked on is never even reached.  Its entire static import
# surface is satisfied: import_chain.py over launcher/engine/filesystem_stdio/
# tier0/vstdlib/shaderapidx9/steam_api reports 1215 imports, 0 holes.
#
# tier0 sizes its thread pool from the processor count and indexes a
# FIXED-SIZE thread-id table.  This machine reports 176 logical processors
# (44 cores x SMT4), and Source's table is far smaller, so the pool overflows
# it during construction.  Nothing about the port is wrong here; the title
# predates machines this wide, exactly like REDengine's refusal of the 2x40
# processor-group view (see 1091500.env).
#
# The cap presents a prefix of the same derivation order -- every processor it
# shows is a real one and the to_unix map is untouched (include/wine/
# cputopology.h).  Both wineserver and every ntdll derive it independently, so
# it must reach both; run-native starts a fresh server per session, which is
# the environment this lever is for.
#
# 64 is the first value tried, chosen to match the classic single-group view
# rather than by measurement.  If Portal 2 still reports the same box at 64,
# the table is smaller than that -- try 32, then 16 -- and record what the
# threshold actually was, because that number is a fact about Source and will
# apply to every Source title on this machine.
#
# NOTE the difference from Quake II, where this same lever was tried and
# FAILED: there the cap took effect (the game reported 64 cores) and the
# exception was thrown anyway, because that engine's PDH counters fail at any
# core count.  A cap that changes the reported number is not evidence it fixed
# anything -- only the title getting further is.
WINE_PPC64LE_CPU_LIMIT=64
